# 0025 - the deaf seat can be mute on the wire too.
#
# S1-Q put one case in scope: a seated player whose link drops for seconds
# and returns. Patch 0016 (-Deaf) drops what arrives, before the ring sees
# it, and the seat keeps sending -- so every deaf run (S1-CM, S1-CO)
# measured the DOWNLINK half of that case and nothing else. The ring's
# one-message-per-second drain was found and fixed there (patch 0024). A
# real outage cuts both ways, and the uplink half has never been modelled:
# the seat's own frames stay in its send arrays unacked; its blind schedule
# re-sends at 2, 4, 8, 16 and 32 s and has nothing between 16 and 32; and
# what its peers can ask for is only what they know is missing, which is
# one id -- the head -- until the seat shows them a newer one.
#
# WHAT THIS IS. A test instrument, fault harness only. With
# P2P_POKER_DEAF_UPLINK=1 beside the 0016 variables, gcc_send_packet drops
# this node's own lossless and lossy group packets during the same window,
# AFTER the ring took them, so entries, ids and the re-send schedule are
# exactly what a cut wire leaves behind; the send reports success, as a cut
# wire does. Handshakes pass, as 0016 lets them pass on the way in. The
# window and its anchor are 0016's, hoisted to file scope so both halves
# read one clock; a send before the anchor exists is not muted, so the
# anchor is not moved by a send. The harness switch is -DeafBothWays, and
# the same commit puts -DeafFor into the list of knobs that refuse a binary
# without the fault harness, because the comment that said 0016 "works in
# every build" was wrong: it sits inside #ifdef P2P_POKER_FAULT_HARNESS.
#
# THE PREDICTION IS BOOKED BEFORE THE RUN, -DeafSeat 3 -DeafAt 240
# -DeafFor 20 -DeafBothWays on the 8+1 rig, and it is the one this
# instrument exists to test. Downlink: repaired within two seconds of the
# window's end, as in split120908-9. Uplink: the seat's frames sent inside
# the window are stuck; the peers' probes draw ONE id per two seconds
# because they know of no gap, and the seat's blind schedule reaches an
# entry added early in the window only at +32 s. So the prediction splits:
# if the seat has something NEW to send right after the window -- its next
# action, a heartbeat, a re-send from the application's own arm -- the peers
# see a newer id, know the gap, and burst it closed within a second, and
# the run shows no certificate and the seat level with the others. If it
# has nothing new to say, because the table is waiting for exactly the
# frame that is stuck, the drain is one id per two seconds, a multi-chunk
# shuffle takes eight to twenty seconds, and the table's thirty-second
# clock -- running since the peers began waiting, inside the window -- runs
# out: a certificate about n3, and the peers' logs showing "Re-sent
# requested packet" answered BY n3 one id every two seconds. The second
# outcome is the one to expect for a shuffle stuck in the window, and it
# would name the sender-side half of S1-CO's defect: nothing pushes an
# unacked backlog when a quiet peer is heard again.

